<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valtellina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Valtellina is the valley of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, running east from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-como">Lake Como</a> towards the Stelvio pass. The Three Leagues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Grisons</a> conquered it in 1512 and ruled it as a subject land; a Catholic rising and massacre of Protestants in 1620, the Sacro Macello, made it a contested corridor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. In 1797 it broke away and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Cisalpine Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valtellina.jpg" alt="Valtellina" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Touring Club Italiano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_lower_Valtellina_from_Colico_to_Sondrio_2008_Touring_Club_Italiano_GR_Lombardia@0379.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Valtellina, also called the Valtelline and in German Veltlin, borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and was a key Alpine pass between northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Control of it was much sought after, particularly during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, because it formed an important part of the Spanish Road. Its most important town is Sondrio. The official language is Italian, and the Valtellinese variety of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> is also spoken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered the region in 16 BCE. By the 5th century it was Christian, with about ten rural churches with baptisteries under the Diocese of Como. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> gained control after 720, and about fifty years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> gave the valley to the Saint Denis Monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, after which it returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Como. In the 16th, 17th and 18th centuries it belonged to the Three Leagues, a mutual-defence region independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> that is now the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a>.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of military and diplomatic struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> powers and local authorities, culminating in the Valtellina War of 1620 to 1626. Between 1618 and 1620 a court of the anti-Habsburg Protestants of the Leagues convicted a number of Catholics, often in absentia, and the Catholic arch-priest Nicolò Rusca of Sondrio died under torture. These judgments led to a conspiracy to drive the Protestants out, led by Giacomo Robustelli of the Planta family.</p><p>On the evening of 18 and 19 July 1620 rebels, supported by Austrian and Italian troops, marched into Tirano and killed Protestants there and then in Teglio, Sondrio and further down the valley. Between 500 and 600 people were killed that night and over the following four days, and nearly all Protestants were driven out of the valley. In February 1623 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> signed the Treaty of Paris to expel the Spanish forces from the territory.</p><p>On 10 October 1797 the Valtellina revolted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a> with French support and joined the Cisalpine Republic. After 1815 it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy–Venetia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> it passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1859 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861. The Ferrovia della Valtellina, electrified in 1902, was the world's first mainline electrified railway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The valley is known for its ski centres, hot spring spas, bresaola, cheeses and wines, made mainly from the Chiavennasca grape, the local Nebbiolo. The Bernina Line of the Rhaetian Railway links Tirano with St. Moritz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. At the Rock Engraving Park in Grosio stands the Rupe Magna, a large rock with more than 5,000 engraved figures dating from between the 4th and 1st millennia BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valtellina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 29 June 1797 Napoleon proclaimed the Cisalpine Republic, merging the Transpadane Republic with territories that soon included the Cispadane Republic, Romagna, the <b>Valtellina</b> and the former Venetian cities of Bergamo, Brescia and Crema.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisalpine-republic">Cisalpine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1512 the leagues conquered the <b>Valtellina</b>, Bormio and Chiavenna, which they ruled as subject lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a></li></ul></section>
